## ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) Migration

Your code uses two **ASP.NET request termination** patterns that rely on `Thread.Abort` internally:

| Old Pattern | Problem in .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Response.End()` | Threw `ThreadAbortException` to halt execution — `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | The `true` (endResponse) param called `Thread.Abort` internally |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | No longer thrown — dead code |

---

### Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core MVC, .NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // Export: Return a FileResult — no Response.Write/End needed
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // ASP.NET Core handles Content-Disposition and streaming for you
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    // Redirect: Return IActionResult — no Thread.Abort needed
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult ProcessAndRedirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login"); // returns immediately, no abort
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch — it no longer exists
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() => /* ... */ "";
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### Key Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---|---|
| **Code after `Response.End()` still runs** | In Web Forms, `Response.End()` aborted execution so downstream code never ran. In Core, you must `return` from the action — no implicit halt. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` becomes dead code** | Remove it entirely. If it contained cleanup logic, move it to a `finally` block. |
| **`Response.Redirect` no longer stops execution** | Always `return Redirect(url)`. Any code after a non-returned `Redirect()` call **will execute**. |
| **`Response.Write` doesn't exist** | Use `File()`, `Content()`, or write to `Response.Body` directly. |
| **`HttpContext.RequestAborted` for long work** | If `GenerateReport()` is expensive, pass `HttpContext.RequestAborted` as a `CancellationToken` so the work stops if the client disconnects. |

---

### Bonus: Cancellation-aware export for long-running reports

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically binds HttpContext.RequestAborted here
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    return File(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data), "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This replaces the old "abort the thread if the client disconnects" behavior with cooperative cancellation — the correct pattern in .NET 8.
